1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Here's how to reverse engineer Timetastic from a written spec.

2
00:00:03,650 --> 00:00:07,550
Claude passed all thirty-two checks, and found seven gaps in my spec.

3
00:00:08,383 --> 00:00:10,389
We gave Claude one Markdown file.

4
00:00:10,431 --> 00:00:13,845
It copies the part of Timetastic a small team actually uses.

5
00:00:13,972 --> 00:00:15,742
A shared calendar of who's off.

6
00:00:15,804 --> 00:00:17,959
Requests that go to each person's approver.

7
00:00:18,023 --> 00:00:20,925
Half days, and public holidays that are never charged.

8
00:00:21,009 --> 00:00:23,628
And a yearly allowance, with capped carry-over.

9
00:00:24,583 --> 00:00:26,909
The core rule is how leave is counted.

10
00:00:27,057 --> 00:00:29,213
Every day has a morning and an afternoon.

11
00:00:29,447 --> 00:00:33,048
Days the person doesn't work, and public holidays, are skipped.

12
00:00:33,107 --> 00:00:38,868
So Maya's request from Thursday, December twenty-fourth to Monday the twenty-eighth costs two days, not five.

13
00:00:38,995 --> 00:00:41,417
Christmas is a holiday, and the weekend is skipped.

14
00:00:41,576 --> 00:00:45,696
And her balance is worked out from her requests every time, so it can never drift.

15
00:00:46,650 --> 00:00:49,232
Claude built it in four phases, one prompt each.

16
00:00:49,338 --> 00:00:53,434
The foundation, with people, teams, leave types and holidays.

17
00:00:53,476 --> 00:00:55,781
Requests, balances and approvals.

18
00:00:55,866 --> 00:00:58,559
Who's out, the team calendar and notifications.

19
00:00:58,734 --> 00:01:01,529
Then year end, export and the calendar feed.

20
00:01:01,678 --> 00:01:06,349
After every phase, the acceptance checks ran in a real browser, from fresh seed data.

21
00:01:07,283 --> 00:01:08,265
Here's what it built.

22
00:01:08,350 --> 00:01:13,641
Who's out shows who's off today and next week, with requests waiting for approval listed apart.

23
00:01:13,854 --> 00:01:17,901
The team calendar marks everyone's leave by day, with pending requests dashed.

24
00:01:17,893 --> 00:01:22,310
The approver sees Approve and Decline on top, with an also-out warning and the balance.

25
00:01:22,501 --> 00:01:26,278
Admin allowances shows every balance, including Priya at minus one day.

26
00:01:26,341 --> 00:01:29,503
And on a phone, a new request previews one and a half days.

27
00:01:30,450 --> 00:01:36,120
In the final run, on a fresh database, all thirty-two checks passed, along with seventy-one automated tests.

28
00:01:37,050 --> 00:01:39,206
But the more useful result is what broke.

29
00:01:39,312 --> 00:01:41,702
The build exposed seven gaps in the spec.

30
00:01:41,786 --> 00:01:43,664
Places where Claude had to guess.

31
00:01:43,877 --> 00:01:48,207
Just viewing last year created allowance rows, which then blocked closing the current year.

32
00:01:48,225 --> 00:01:51,405
The team calendar didn't say whether a holiday or a day off wins.

33
00:01:51,511 --> 00:01:55,330
And a pending request that had already started had nowhere to show on Who's out.

34
00:01:55,479 --> 00:01:57,582
Every gap is now fixed in the spec.

35
00:01:58,517 --> 00:02:00,928
Passing every check isn't the same as production-ready.

36
00:02:01,077 --> 00:02:07,883
We didn't test production sign-in, sending email, subscribing to the calendar feed, the hourly job on a real scheduler, or hosting.

37
00:02:07,946 --> 00:02:10,697
Those are yours to check before a team relies on it.

38
00:02:11,650 --> 00:02:13,122
So is it worth building?

39
00:02:13,314 --> 00:02:15,192
Honestly, not to save money.

40
00:02:15,319 --> 00:02:19,885
Timetastic Business is a dollar fifty per user per month, and Pro is two dollars fifty.

41
00:02:19,970 --> 00:02:23,317
For twenty people on Business, that's three hundred and sixty dollars a year.

42
00:02:23,336 --> 00:02:28,478
If you need holiday lists for many regions, accruals, or Slack and Teams, buy Timetastic.

43
00:02:28,584 --> 00:02:32,259
Build it when you want your own rules and data, next to your other internal tools.

44
00:02:33,183 --> 00:02:34,656
Everything is in one file.

45
00:02:34,762 --> 00:02:42,251
The data model, seventeen rules, seed data, four phase prompts, and all thirty-two checks, with the seven fixes already in.

46
00:02:42,967 --> 00:02:46,408
The full spec is free at personal software factory dot com.

47
00:02:46,458 --> 00:02:48,538
If you build it, tell me what broke.

48
00:02:48,588 --> 00:02:49,011
I'm Sam.

49
00:02:49,061 --> 00:02:50,017
See you tomorrow.

